WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.220
Film Courage: So I’m confused about this?

00:00:01.220 --> 00:00:06.359
I just have to ask (and that is) one thing
a director does not want in their movie is

00:00:06.359 --> 00:00:07.359
acting?

00:00:07.359 --> 00:00:09.340
Mark W. Travis: Yes.

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:13.460
I have said this and maybe it upsets some
people.

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:20.660
Along with one of the most dangerous things
on the set is an actor with a plan.

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:23.060
But I also say what we don’t want on our
movie is acting.

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:24.630
But by acting…“acting!”

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:28.230
I mean pretending.

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:32.190
A manufactured performance.

00:00:32.190 --> 00:00:36.640
Now it’s true, there are actors who have
won Academy Awards for what I call acting

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:44.600
because it is all so well-crafted and presented
and considered and worked out.

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:50.519
I think what we really want to see, what I
want to see…I’m not going to be startled

00:00:50.519 --> 00:00:52.390
by a brilliant “performance.”

00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:58.370
I am going to be startled by an extraordinary
human being who reveals themselves authentically

00:00:58.370 --> 00:00:59.390
on screen.

00:00:59.390 --> 00:01:06.430
When I see something that I wasn’t expecting,
what I feel generally is coming genuinely

00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:10.180
from that character that was not planned.

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:11.340
That was not considered.

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:13.670
That was not honed sharply.

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:15.571
It just came out.

00:01:15.571 --> 00:01:16.571
That’s what I want.

00:01:16.571 --> 00:01:20.650
That’s what I see in BLUE JASMINE or MANCHESTER
BY THE SEA.

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:23.700
I see moments like that that I just find chilling.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:25.640
That’s what I want.

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:30.770
Now I’ve seen a lot of performers that are
very manufactured.

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:37.970
There are some films and some filmmakers where
that kind of acting is what’s required.

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:40.080
You watch Wes Anderson’s work and stuff
like that.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:41.080
That’s what’s required.

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:42.080
That’s his style.

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:45.170
Nothing wrong with that, that’s the style
of that work.

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:48.770
You could even go back to Kabuki Theatre,
that’s a style.

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:53.810
What I am saying because what I want when
I see a movie is that I want to feel that

00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:58.310
I’m in the presence of these huge genuine
human beings and experiencing them deeply

00:01:58.310 --> 00:02:01.640
and profoundly and then it’s not pretense.

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:05.810
It’s not calculated, it’s genuine.

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:14.359
So, no acting, please…which means…you
have to stop directing.

00:02:14.359 --> 00:02:16.999
Directors have to stop directing so actors
will stop acting.

00:02:16.999 --> 00:02:22.069
Which means that’s why directors switch
to interrogation and form a relationship with

00:02:22.069 --> 00:02:23.819
the character.

00:02:23.819 --> 00:02:28.799
Rather than the director telling the actor
“Here’s what I want you to do.”

00:02:28.799 --> 00:02:32.880
The director is stimulating the character
to go in and find what they do.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:37.950
Film Courage: Okay, so instead of Casey Affleck’s
character in MANCHESTER BY THE SEA, if I’m

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:39.489
his director, saying “You know what, Casey?

00:02:39.489 --> 00:02:43.749
I want you to look really depressed and I
want you to be in this room with no furniture.”

00:02:43.749 --> 00:02:47.079
Instead I’m going up to him and saying “You’re
life is bad.

00:02:47.079 --> 00:02:52.120
It’s fallen apart and you’re here alone
in this dingy room?”

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:53.709
Mark W. Travis: Yes.

00:02:53.709 --> 00:02:58.379
I’d be saying to his character (I’ve forgotten
the character’s name) but saying to Casey

00:02:58.379 --> 00:03:02.739
as his character “You’re alone in this
dingy room….what the heck are you going

00:03:02.739 --> 00:03:03.739
to do?

00:03:03.739 --> 00:03:04.739
What are you going to do?

00:03:04.739 --> 00:03:07.549
Do you realize that everybody in Manchester
By The Sea hates you?

00:03:07.549 --> 00:03:08.549
Do you realize that?

00:03:08.549 --> 00:03:10.529
And you’re going to go back there?

00:03:10.529 --> 00:03:11.930
What are you thinking?”

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:15.870
Now I am getting him into the chaos of the
character.

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:18.290
Then I say “Okay, let’s do the scene.”

00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:21.680
Now we are doing the scene now he’s totally
in the chaos of the character and trying to

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:28.419
fight through that chaos while he’s trying
to fight his way through a scene simultaneously.

00:03:28.419 --> 00:03:31.650
Sort of like we all do in life every day.

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:35.849
We fight through our own chaos while we are
trying to get through the daily chaos.

00:03:35.849 --> 00:03:38.290
He’s not thinking about acting.

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:40.519
He’s thinking about survival.

00:03:40.519 --> 00:03:42.150
Seriously, how do I get through this?

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:43.750
How do I get through this moment?

00:03:43.750 --> 00:03:48.310
How do I quiet the voices in my head that
came through the interrogation?

00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:53.819
The voices are going mad in his head and he’s
trying to quiet those down or sort through

00:03:53.819 --> 00:03:58.430
those while he’s trying to deal with whoever,
this lady who needs some plumbing done.

00:03:58.430 --> 00:04:01.430
Or whatever it is, that’s what he’s dealing
with.

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:06.969
He’s dealing with only what the character
is dealing with.

00:04:06.969 --> 00:04:09.400
Not with what Casey Affleck is dealing with.

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:12.640
Casey Affleck (in that moment) does not matter.

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:16.220
And quite honestly, I can tell you as a director,
at that moment when I was directing him, I

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:17.810
don’t care what Casey Affleck is thinking.

00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:19.870
I don’t care what he is thinking about the
character.

00:04:19.870 --> 00:04:21.080
I don’t want him thinking about the character.

00:04:21.080 --> 00:04:23.389
I only care about the character.

00:04:23.389 --> 00:04:28.889
I’m there to film the character, not to
film an acting performance.

00:04:28.889 --> 00:04:32.750
It’s a fine line but it’s a significant
line.

